<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Simplifying factorial fractions by expanding and cancelling</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Simplify each factorial fraction by completing the step-by-step expansion and cancellation.</p>

<p><b>Part A: Simplify \(\displaystyle \frac{{@n1@}!}{{@k1@}!}\)</b></p>
<ol>
  <li>Expand the numerator until it reaches the factorial in the denominator:<br>
  \(\displaystyle {@n1@}! = {@n1@} \times\) [[input:ans1]] \(\times\) [[input:ans2]]
  <div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
  <div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
  </li>
  <li>Substitute into the fraction and cancel the common factor \({@k1@}!\) from the numerator and denominator:<br>
  \(\displaystyle \frac{{@n1@}!}{{@k1@}!} = \frac{{@n1@} \times {@m1@} \times {@k1@}!}{{@k1@}!} = {@n1@} \times {@m1@}\)</li>
  <li>Multiply the remaining factors to find the simplified value:<br>
  \(\displaystyle {@n1@} \times {@m1@} =\) [[input:ans3]]
  <div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
  </li>
</ol>

<p><b>Part B: Simplify \(\displaystyle \frac{{@n2@}!}{{@k2@}!}\)</b></p>
<ol>
  <li>Expand the numerator until it reaches the factorial in the denominator:<br>
  \(\displaystyle {@n2@}! = {@n2@} \times\) [[input:ans4]] \(\times\) [[input:ans5]]
  <div>[[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>
  <div>[[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</div>
  </li>
  <li>Substitute into the fraction and cancel the common factor \({@k2@}!\) from the numerator and denominator:<br>
  \(\displaystyle \frac{{@n2@}!}{{@k2@}!} = \frac{{@n2@} \times {@m2@} \times {@k2@}!}{{@k2@}!} = {@n2@} \times {@m2@}\)</li>
  <li>Multiply the remaining factors to find the simplified value:<br>
  \(\displaystyle {@n2@} \times {@m2@} =\) [[input:ans6]]
  <div>[[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]]</div>
  </li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>To simplify a fraction of factorials \(\displaystyle \frac{n!}{k!}\) where \(n &gt; k\), expand the larger factorial in the numerator only until it matches the smaller factorial in the denominator, then cancel the common factorial term.</p>

<p><b>Part A: Simplify \(\displaystyle \frac{{@n1@}!}{{@k1@}!}\)</b></p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \frac{{@n1@}!}{{@k1@}!} = \frac{{@n1@} \times {@m1@} \times {@k1@}!}{{@k1@}!}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">Cancel the common factor \({@k1@}!\) from the top and bottom:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle = {@n1@} \times {@m1@} = {@ta3@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \frac{{@n1@}!}{{@k1@}!} = {@ta3@} }\)</p>

<p><b>Part B: Simplify \(\displaystyle \frac{{@n2@}!}{{@k2@}!}\)</b></p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \frac{{@n2@}!}{{@k2@}!} = \frac{{@n2@} \times {@m2@} \times {@k2@}!}{{@k2@}!}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">Cancel the common factor \({@k2@}!\) from the top and bottom:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle = {@n2@} \times {@m2@} = {@ta6@}\)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \frac{{@n2@}!}{{@k2@}!} = {@ta6@} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
